22:38:51 [fda7] Use this conceptual-difficulty guidance to choose WHAT the questions test (content and reasoning level):
22:38:51 [fda7] --- CONCEPT GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
22:38:51 [fda7] Concept level (ages 8): School-age conceptual reasoning involving explicit category relations, cross-classification, hierarchical reasoning, causal explanation, food-origin/processing reasoning, problem-solution reasoning, and structured narrative understanding.
22:38:51 [fda7] Wording: Questions can be longer and can ask for explanation, comparison, or reasoning, but should remain concrete and contextually grounded. Phrase every question as ONE simple spoken clause a child would hear in conversation; split complex ideas into the question frames below instead of packing them into one sentence.
22:38:51 [fda7] Question length: 8-14 words when expressive language level supports it.
22:38:51 [fda7] Avoid: fully abstract reasoning without concrete context; adult-level nutrition or biology mechanisms; technical food production; highly ambiguous category exceptions; complex social inference without story support; academic connector phrases such as 'in terms of', 'with respect to', 'in what way'; two asks packed into one question (e.g. 'How are X and Y alike in terms of what they are made from?' — use 'What are X and Y both made from?' instead)
22:38:51 [fda7] Use these simple question frames (one clause each): How are X and Y alike? | Why does X belong to this category? | Can X belong to two categories? | What is X made from? | Why did the character do that? | What happened after X? | What should the character do next?
22:38:51 [fda7] Target these concepts (vary across the question set):
22:38:51 [fda7] - taxonomic thematic relation: Ask how two items are related by category or by everyday use/event context. e.g. How are apples and bananas alike?
22:38:51 [fda7] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Why can an apple be both a fruit and a snack?
22:38:51 [fda7] - superordinate subordinate reasoning: Ask whether an item is a kind of another category, or whether one category is broader than another. e.g. What kind of animal is a poodle?
22:38:51 [fda7] - multi attribute categorization: Ask the child to reason using two or more features. e.g. Why do umbrellas help in rain?
22:38:51 [fda7] - origin and processing reasoning: Ask about natural source, animal source, ingredient source, or simple processing pathway. e.g. How are oranges changed into orange juice?
22:38:51 [fda7] - ingredient to food pathway: Ask simple ingredient-to-product relationships. e.g. What is bread made from?
22:38:51 [fda7] - causal explanation: Ask why something happens or why an object/action is useful. e.g. Why do people use umbrellas when it rains?
22:38:51 [fda7] - problem solution reasoning: Ask what someone should do when a familiar problem occurs. e.g. What can you do if a pencil breaks?
22:38:51 [fda7] - narrative goal attempt outcome: Ask about what a character wanted, what they did, what happened, and how they felt. e.g. What did the child want?
22:38:51 [fda7] - prediction inference: Ask the child to make a simple prediction or inference from a familiar context. e.g. What might happen when clouds get dark?
22:38:51 [fda7] - event sequence and planning: Ask whether an action happens before, after, or to prepare for a familiar event. e.g. What do we do to our hands before eating?
22:38:51 [fda7] Example question at this level: How are apples and bananas alike?
22:38:51 [fda7] Example question at this level: Is an apple both fruit and food?
22:38:51 [fda7] Example question at this level: Is a poodle a kind of dog?
